WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.930 --> 00:00:02.860
Film Courage: What does character development
entail?

00:00:02.860 --> 00:00:03.860
Are you writing a backstory?

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:04.970
Scott Myers, DePaul University Professor/Screenwriter/Creator
Gointothestory.blcklst(dot)com: To me that’s

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:05.999
everything, it’s everything.

00:00:05.999 --> 00:00:09.080
Here is another one of my sayings, you ready?

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:13.100
“Begin with character, end with character,
find the story in between.”

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:17.620
I don’t know what paradigm people have in
terms of story structure.

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:19.500
How you get there is critical.

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:23.170
And so you start with your characters, that’s
my take.

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:25.860
Again, there is not one right way to do this.

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:33.170
I’m just saying that this is a smart way
to go about it because the characters, it’s

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:34.170
there story.

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:39.610
If we’re writing a story, we are assuming
that in some magical, mystical way that story

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:43.329
universe exists, those characters exist.

00:00:43.329 --> 00:00:48.480
And if it’s their story, they know it better
than we do and so character development is

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:49.480
about getting curious.

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:50.740
It’s about asking questions.

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:53.670
It’s about spending time with the characters.

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:59.899
And so the typical things we see like biographies
and questionnaires, those are great.

00:00:59.899 --> 00:01:03.899
I call those indirect engagement exercises
because you are reflecting on the character,

00:01:03.899 --> 00:01:09.240
you are thinking about the character, as opposed
to direct engagement exercises.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:17.640
So for example what if I have a character
in mind, I’m going to imagine I am a psychiatrist.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:22.900
This character is my patient and if they’re
reticent to talk to me I can even go one step

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:23.900
further.

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:28.670
They have been court-appointed to appear with
me as a psychiatrist and they have to answer

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:33.240
my questions and if they don’t they will
stay stuck in the situation until they do.

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:37.650
So you just create a situation in which (like
I was saying earlier) you just ask questions,

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:38.650
you get curious.

00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:42.480
That was a lesson I learned when I was an
Air Force brat, you just keep asking people

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:44.380
questions and getting them talking.

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:47.280
And so you just type, you let it go.

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:52.479
You can even do something that is a little
bit spiritual I suppose you’d say in a way

00:01:52.479 --> 00:01:58.659
where you are going to do either a monologue
or a stream of consciousness with a character.

00:01:58.659 --> 00:02:02.729
It’s sort of like a Vulcan mind meld, you
know like in Star Trek where you get a character

00:02:02.729 --> 00:02:08.550
in your mind and you do some deep breathing
you get yourself so you are transitioning

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:13.220
out of the busy world and you are going to
be here now with this character, you set your

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:18.249
timer for like 15 minutes and you put your
fingers on the keyboard or pen on paper, you

00:02:18.249 --> 00:02:21.250
close your eyes and you just type and you
just type.

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:25.329
And your mind will go “I’ve got to get
groceries or the cat…(whatever).”

00:02:25.329 --> 00:02:27.930
Let that go, you don’t get attached to it.

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:33.159
You just keep coming back to this character
and you get done with this.

00:02:33.159 --> 00:02:37.409
So you’ve got all these words that you’ve
typed out some of them are like dialogue like

00:02:37.409 --> 00:02:40.709
a monologue and some of them are just like
stream of consciousness.

00:02:40.709 --> 00:02:45.499
80% of it may seem like gobbledygook to you,
maybe 90%?

00:02:45.499 --> 00:02:52.120
But 20% or 10%, there is something really
interesting that popped out there and that’s

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:58.599
getting in touch with the characters in a
subconscious level and they start to talk

00:02:58.599 --> 00:02:59.599
to you.

00:02:59.599 --> 00:03:01.159
I’ve seen this happen time and time again.

00:03:01.159 --> 00:03:07.089
I created a prep class 10 years ago that I
teach online (a 6-week workshop) taking a

00:03:07.089 --> 00:03:10.599
story from concept to outline.

00:03:10.599 --> 00:03:14.730
The first four weeks are all character development,
the second week is brain storming.

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:21.900
That’s all they do it brainstorm and I’ve
had hundreds of people tell me that was the

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:25.339
most eye-opening experience for them was doing
that type of work.

00:03:25.339 --> 00:03:30.809
Again, the characters exists, we act on that
belief and we reach out to them and get them

00:03:30.809 --> 00:03:33.510
talking to us so the character.

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:37.349
So the character development, those are some
great examples for character development.

00:03:37.349 --> 00:03:41.859
What can happen out of all of that is if you
identify a protagonist in particular their

00:03:41.859 --> 00:03:49.260
state of disunity where they are disconnected
from their authentic nature and that can inform

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:54.370
(if you know what the disunity state is) that
can inform where the unity state is.

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:56.700
And so now they are not going to just jump
there.

00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:03.079
Then you’re going to say Okay these plot
elements and the characters with whom they

00:04:03.079 --> 00:04:09.400
intersect they are going to deconstruct their
old ways of being because they don’t work

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:10.989
out here in the new world.

00:04:10.989 --> 00:04:13.559
But in the process of that they are going
to uncover their need.

00:04:13.559 --> 00:04:15.290
Their need is going to start to emerge.

00:04:15.290 --> 00:04:16.340
This is very Jungian.

00:04:16.340 --> 00:04:21.579
Jung talks about (he says) “When an inner
situation is not made conscious (and he’s

00:04:21.579 --> 00:04:26.870
talking about like conflict or inner conflict)
it happens outside as fate.”

00:04:26.870 --> 00:04:33.340
Now I don’t know if that’s in the real
world, but that’s the protagonist story.

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:39.990
The protagonist starts off in a state of disunity
and what happens is the plot is informed by

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:41.360
that, there’s a synergy between it.

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:42.840
It’s not just these random events.

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:47.760
It’s like these characters that they intersect
with and the events that they go though that’s

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:52.479
going to provide the basis of their transformation
and so there disunity that gets deconstructed,

00:04:52.479 --> 00:04:57.139
well now this stuff starts to come out into
the light of consciousness (their need) and

00:04:57.139 --> 00:05:02.000
then they move into reconstruction, now they
are moving into their new mode of being and

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:04.660
eventually toward unity.

00:05:04.660 --> 00:05:10.490
So you can take that raw material that you
get with your characters and see how that

00:05:10.490 --> 00:05:14.490
starts to shape the nature of the plot (the
story structure).

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:19.050
And it’s not just plugging this into ‘this
has to happen on page 25 - the break in the

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:20.050
Act 2.’

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:23.949
No, you are doing it through much more of
an organic fashion with your characters and

00:05:23.949 --> 00:05:27.310
letting them inform you what the structure
will be.

00:05:27.310 --> 00:05:33.169
The final value of that is that you’re writing
multi-layered, richly textured characters

00:05:33.169 --> 00:05:35.469
who actors want to play.

00:05:35.469 --> 00:05:38.419
So character development to me is like everything.

00:05:38.419 --> 00:05:40.259
It’s absolutely critical.

